      ],
      "source": "https://www.lakesnwoods.com/Lengby.htm",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name evolved from Lindby to Lengby when the Great Northern Railway arrived in 1898, adding the Swedish suffix meaning “village.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lakesnwoods.com/Lengby.htm",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1980, a local woman named Jean Hilliard survived six hours in subzero temperatures, a story later featured on Unsolved Mysteries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lengby%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lengby sits at 1,385 feet above sea level, offering a modest elevation within the North Dakota–Minnesota corridor.",
